<h1>Derivation of the Fenske Equation and the Underwood Equations for Minimum Reflux</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Minimum number of equilibrium stages at total reflux and minimum reflux ratio at an infinite number of stages, derived step by step from equilibrium relations and material balances</p>

<p>Two limiting conditions bound the design of a distillation column. At one limit, operation at total reflux with no feed introduction and no product withdrawal gives the smallest possible number of equilibrium stages for a specified separation; the Fenske equation evaluates that minimum stage count from the product compositions and the relative volatility alone [1]. At the other limit, operation at the minimum reflux ratio requires an infinite number of equilibrium stages; the Underwood equations evaluate that minimum reflux ratio from the feed composition, the feed thermal condition, the distillate composition, and the relative volatilities [2, 3]. The following sections derive both results from stated first principles. Every algebraic operation is shown, every assumption is stated at the point where the derivation first depends on the assumption, and a single worked example, carried through both derivations and checked by an independent graphical construction, closes the development.</p>

