<h2 id="setting">Physical setting and common assumptions</h2>
<p>The column separates a feed into a distillate and a bottoms through a rectifying section above the feed stage and a stripping section below the feed stage, with a condenser at the top and a reboiler at the bottom. Four assumptions are common to both derivations, and each later assumption is stated separately in the section that requires the assumption.</p>
<ol>
<li>Assumption C1, steady state: no accumulation term appears in any material balance.</li>
<li>Assumption C2, equilibrium stages: the liquid stream and the vapour stream leaving any stage are in phase equilibrium, so the distribution coefficient of Table 1 relates the two stream compositions on every stage. Real trays approach equilibrium-stage behaviour through an efficiency correction that lies outside both derivations.</li>
<li>Assumption C3, overall material balance: the component molar flow rate of any component in the feed equals the sum of the component molar flow rates of that component in the two products. In the symbols of Table 1, the overall component balance states that the feed component flow equals the distillate component flow plus the bottoms component flow:</li>
</ol>
$$f_{i} = d_{i} + b_{i} \tag{1}$$
<li>Assumption C4, summation of mole fractions: the mole fractions in any single stream sum to one. Applied to the vapour stream leaving a stage, the summation gives the vapour-phase closure used at several points in both derivations.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="fenske">Derivation of the Fenske equation</h2>
<p>Specific assumptions. Assumption F1, total reflux: no feed is introduced and no product is withdrawn, so the liquid molar flow rate and the vapour molar flow rate between any two adjacent stages are equal, and all material leaving the top of the column returns as reflux while all material leaving the bottom returns as boilup. Assumption F2, constant relative volatility in the basic form: the relative volatility of the light key with respect to the heavy key has the same value on every stage. The variable-relative-volatility case is treated after the basic form, by replacement of the constant value with the stage-averaged value, and is an approximation rather than an exact extension.</p>
<p>Step F1: equilibrium ratio on a single stage. The equilibrium relation of Assumption C2, applied to the light key on stage \(j\) and then to the heavy key on the same stage, gives two equations:</p>
$$y_{\mathrm{LK},j} = K_{\mathrm{LK},j} \, x_{\mathrm{LK},j} \qquad \text{and} \qquad y_{\mathrm{HK},j} = K_{\mathrm{HK},j} \, x_{\mathrm{HK},j} \tag{2}$$
<p>Division of the first equation of Eq. (2) by the second equation of Eq. (2), with collection of the distribution coefficients in one quotient and of the compositions in another quotient, gives:</p>
$$\frac{y_{\mathrm{LK},j}}{y_{\mathrm{HK},j}} = \frac{K_{\mathrm{LK},j}}{K_{\mathrm{HK},j}} \; \frac{x_{\mathrm{LK},j}}{x_{\mathrm{HK},j}} \tag{3}$$
<p>The quotient of distribution coefficients in Eq. (3) is, by the definition in Table 1 with the heavy key as reference component, the relative volatility on stage \(j\). Substitution of that definition into Eq. (3) gives the single-stage ratio relation:</p>
$$\frac{y_{\mathrm{LK},j}}{y_{\mathrm{HK},j}} = \alpha_{j} \, \frac{x_{\mathrm{LK},j}}{x_{\mathrm{HK},j}} \tag{4}$$
<p>Physical reading of Eq. (4): one equilibrium stage multiplies the light-key to heavy-key ratio of a stream by the relative volatility. A relative volatility of one would give no change of ratio on the stage and no separation; the entire separation in both derivations is the accumulation of the per-stage multiplication of Eq. (4).</p>
<p>Step F2: passing-stream equality at total reflux. Consider the two streams passing each other between stage \(j\) and stage \(j + 1\): the vapour stream rising from stage \(j\) and the liquid stream descending from stage \(j + 1\). At total reflux, by Assumption F1, the two streams have equal molar flow rates, and a component balance over the part of the column above the cut between the two stages contains no product term, so the component molar flow rates in the two passing streams are equal. Equal total flow rates and equal component flow rates give equal compositions, for every component:</p>
$$y_{i,j} = x_{i,j+1} \tag{5}$$
<p>Equation (5) is the total-reflux operating relation. On a composition diagram for a binary mixture, Eq. (5) states that the operating line coincides with the diagonal line on which the vapour composition equals the liquid composition.</p>
<p>Step F3: stage-to-stage recurrence for the composition ratio. Application of Eq. (5) to the light key and to the heavy key separately, followed by division of the light-key result by the heavy-key result, converts the vapour ratio in Eq. (4) into the liquid ratio on the stage above:</p>
$$\frac{x_{\mathrm{LK},j+1}}{x_{\mathrm{HK},j+1}} = \alpha_{j} \, \frac{x_{\mathrm{LK},j}}{x_{\mathrm{HK},j}} \tag{6}$$
<p>Substitution of the liquid composition ratio of Table 1 into Eq. (6) shortens the recurrence to multiplication by the relative volatility on the stage:</p>
$$\rho_{j+1} = \alpha_{j} \, \rho_{j} \tag{7}$$
<p>Step F4: end conditions. At the bottom of the column, the liquid stream leaving stage 1 is the bottoms product of the separation under consideration, so the liquid composition ratio on stage 1 equals the bottoms composition ratio:</p>
$$\rho_{1} = \frac{x_{B,\mathrm{LK}}}{x_{B,\mathrm{HK}}} \tag{8}$$
<p>At the top of the column, the distillate composition equals the composition of the vapour stream leaving the top stage: with a total condenser, the condensate has the same composition as the vapour entering the condenser; with a partial condenser, the distillate is itself that vapour, and the partial condenser is counted as the top stage. The equilibrium relation applied once more on the top stage therefore relates the distillate composition ratio to the liquid composition ratio on the top stage through the same per-stage multiplication as Eq. (4):</p>
$$\frac{x_{D,\mathrm{LK}}}{x_{D,\mathrm{HK}}} = \alpha_{N} \, \rho_{N} \tag{9}$$
<p>In Eq. (9), the number of equilibrium stages appears because the top stage is stage \(N\) under the counting convention of Table 1.</p>
<p>Step F5: multiplication over all stages. Repeated application of the recurrence of Eq. (7) from stage 1 to stage \(N - 1\), where \(N - 1\) is the number of stage-to-stage steps between the bottom stage and the top stage, expresses the liquid composition ratio on the top stage as the bottoms composition ratio multiplied by the product of the relative volatilities on stages 1 through \(N - 1\). Substitution of that product into Eq. (9) adds the relative volatility of the top stage and gives the complete product over all stages:</p>
$$\frac{x_{D,\mathrm{LK}}}{x_{D,\mathrm{HK}}} = \left( \prod_{j=1}^{N} \alpha_{j} \right) \frac{x_{B,\mathrm{LK}}}{x_{B,\mathrm{HK}}} \tag{10}$$
<p>The counting in Eq. (10) is the point at which stage-counting errors most commonly enter the Fenske equation: the exponent counts every equilibrium stage once, the reboiler contributes one factor through the recurrence start at stage 1, a partial condenser contributes one factor as the top stage, and a total condenser contributes no factor because no equilibrium separation occurs in a total condenser.</p>
<p>Step F6: constant relative volatility and solution for the stage count. Under Assumption F2, every factor in the product of Eq. (10) equals the constant relative volatility, so the product of \(N\) equal factors is the constant relative volatility raised to the power \(N\). At total reflux the stage count for the specified separation is the minimum stage count, because total reflux is the operating condition of maximum internal traffic and therefore of maximum separation per stage sequence; replacement of the stage count by the minimum stage count gives:</p>
$$\frac{x_{D,\mathrm{LK}}}{x_{D,\mathrm{HK}}} \; \frac{x_{B,\mathrm{HK}}}{x_{B,\mathrm{LK}}} = \alpha^{N_{\min}} \tag{11}$$
<p>Taking the natural logarithm of both sides of Eq. (11), applying the logarithm power rule to the right-hand side, and dividing by the natural logarithm of the constant relative volatility gives the Fenske equation [1, 4]:</p>
$$N_{\min} = \frac{\ln \left[ \left( x_{D,\mathrm{LK}}/x_{D,\mathrm{HK}} \right) \left( x_{B,\mathrm{HK}}/x_{B,\mathrm{LK}} \right) \right]}{\ln \alpha} \tag{12}$$
<p>Any logarithm base may replace the natural logarithm in Eq. (12), because the change-of-base factor appears in both the numerator and the denominator and cancels. The argument of the numerator logarithm is a product of two enrichment ratios: the light-key to heavy-key ratio in the distillate, which is large for a sharp separation, and the heavy-key to light-key ratio in the bottoms, which is likewise large. Each factor of relative volatility per stage must supply the total enrichment, so the minimum stage count grows logarithmically with the required enrichment and decreases as the relative volatility increases.</p>
<p>Step F7: binary form. In a binary mixture of the light key and the heavy key, the heavy-key mole fraction in any stream equals one minus the light-key mole fraction in the same stream. Writing the light-key mole fractions in the distillate and in the bottoms with the generic composition symbols of Table 1 specialised to the two products gives the common binary form of the Fenske equation:</p>
$$N_{\min} = \frac{\ln \left[ \left( x_{D}/(1 - x_{D}) \right) \left( (1 - x_{B})/x_{B} \right) \right]}{\ln \alpha} \tag{13}$$
<p>In Eq. (13), the distillate mole fraction of the light key is written without a component subscript because the binary context identifies the component, and the bottoms mole fraction of the light key is written in the same convention; both quantities are the corresponding entries of Table 1 for the light key.</p>
<p>Step F8: variable relative volatility. When the relative volatility varies from stage to stage, Eq. (10) remains exact for equilibrium stages at total reflux, and the product of stage relative volatilities may be written as a power of the stage-averaged relative volatility, defined as the geometric mean of the stage values:</p>
$$\alpha_{\mathrm{avg}} = \left( \prod_{j=1}^{N} \alpha_{j} \right)^{1/N} \tag{14}$$
<p>Replacement of the constant relative volatility in Eq. (12) by the stage-averaged relative volatility of Eq. (14) gives the variable-volatility form of the Fenske equation. Evaluation of the geometric mean requires the stage-by-stage values, which are not known before the column is calculated; the standard approximation replaces the geometric mean over all stages by the geometric mean of the top-stage value and the bottom-stage value [4]:</p>
$$\alpha_{\mathrm{avg}} \approx \sqrt{\alpha_{\mathrm{top}} \, \alpha_{\mathrm{bot}}} \tag{15}$$
<p>Equation (15) is exact when the logarithm of the relative volatility varies linearly with stage position, and is otherwise an approximation whose error grows with the curvature of the temperature and composition dependence of the relative volatility.</p>
<p>Step F9: multicomponent form and component distribution. The recurrence of Steps F1 to F5 used only the ratio of two components, so the same derivation applies to any component paired with any reference component. Application to component \(i\) paired with reference component \(r\), with the stage-averaged relative volatility of the pair used in place of the constant relative volatility, and conversion of the end composition ratios into component molar flow rate ratios through the definitions of Table 1 (the distillate flow rate cancels between the two components within the distillate, and the bottoms flow rate cancels in the same way within the bottoms), gives the multicomponent Fenske relation:</p>
$$\frac{d_{i}}{b_{i}} = \alpha_{i,r,\mathrm{avg}}^{N_{\min}} \; \frac{d_{r}}{b_{r}} \tag{16}$$
<p>In Eq. (16), the stage-averaged relative volatility of component \(i\) with respect to reference component \(r\) is written with the subscript avg attached to the relative-volatility symbol of Table 1. Equation (16) is used in two directions: with the minimum stage count obtained from the key pair through Eq. (12), Eq. (16) estimates the split of every non-key component between the products; and the sensitivity of Eq. (16) to the stage count explains why the split of a component far in volatility from the reference component is estimated much less accurately than the split of the keys, because a small error in the minimum stage count is raised to the full power in the distribution of a distant component.</p>
<p>Scope and limitations of the Fenske result. The result is independent of the feed composition, of the feed thermal condition, and of the feed stage location, because total reflux admits no feed. The result is a minimum: any finite reflux ratio requires more stages for the same separation, and total reflux itself withdraws no product, so the minimum stage count is a bound for design and a starting value for rigorous calculation, not an operating point. Failure of the constant-relative-volatility assumption in the basic form, strong non-ideality, or a relative volatility close to one (for which the denominator of Eq. (12) approaches zero and the minimum stage count grows without bound) limits the accuracy of the estimate.</p>

<h2 id="underwood">Derivation of the Underwood equations</h2>
<p>Minimum reflux and the pinch zone. As the reflux ratio is reduced at fixed product specifications, the separation achieved per stage falls and the number of stages required rises. At the minimum reflux ratio the number of stages required is infinite. The stages accumulate in a pinch zone: a region, adjacent to the feed stage in each section for the separations treated by the Underwood method, in which the liquid composition and the vapour composition cease to change from stage to stage, because the operating relation and the equilibrium relation are satisfied by the same pair of compositions at every stage of the region. Further stages in the pinch zone therefore accomplish no further separation. The constant compositions in the two pinch zones are the pinch compositions defined in Table 1. The derivation evaluates the conditions that the pinch compositions must satisfy in each section and combines the two sets of conditions through the overall balance and the feed condition [2, 3, 5].</p>
<p>Specific assumptions. Assumption U1, constant molar overflow: the liquid molar flow rate and the vapour molar flow rate are constant within the rectifying section and constant, at generally different values, within the stripping section, because the molar latent heats are approximately equal, sensible-heat effects and heats of mixing are negligible, and the column is adiabatic. Assumption U2, constant relative volatilities: the relative volatility of every component with respect to the reference component has the same value throughout the column; the reference component is the heavy key, so the relative volatility of the heavy key is one. Assumption U3, pinch zones in both sections at the feed: at the minimum reflux condition a pinch zone of constant composition exists in the rectifying section and a pinch zone of constant composition exists in the stripping section. Assumption U4, common pinch parameter, is stated at Step U9, the first step that depends on the assumption. Assumption U5, product compositions at minimum reflux: the distillate composition appearing in the final equations is the distillate composition at the minimum reflux condition; in design use the specified distillate composition of the distributing components is inserted as an approximation, and the approximation is one source of error of the method.</p>
<p>Step U1: equilibrium in the rectifying pinch zone. Because the composition does not change from stage to stage in the pinch zone, the equilibrium relation of Assumption C2 applies to the pinch compositions themselves:</p>
$$y_{i}^{\mathrm{R}} = K_{i,\mathrm{R}} \, x_{i}^{\mathrm{R}} \tag{17}$$
<p>Step U2: component balance over the top envelope in the pinch zone. A component material balance over an envelope enclosing the condenser and the column down to a cut in the rectifying pinch zone relates the passing pinch streams to the distillate component flow: the component molar flow rate entering the envelope in the vapour stream equals the component molar flow rate leaving in the liquid stream plus the component molar flow rate leaving in the distillate:</p>
$$V y_{i}^{\mathrm{R}} = L x_{i}^{\mathrm{R}} + d_{i} \tag{18}$$
<p>Step U3: solution for the pinch liquid composition. Substitution of Eq. (17) into Eq. (18) replaces the pinch vapour composition by the product of the distribution coefficient and the pinch liquid composition. Collection of the two terms containing the pinch liquid composition and division by the collected coefficient gives the pinch liquid composition in terms of the distillate component flow and the section flow rates:</p>
$$x_{i}^{\mathrm{R}} = \frac{d_{i}}{V K_{i,\mathrm{R}} - L} \tag{19}$$
<p>Step U4: vapour-phase closure in the rectifying pinch zone. Summation of Eq. (17) over all components, with the pinch vapour compositions summing to one by Assumption C4 and each pinch vapour composition then replaced through Eqs. (17) and (19), gives:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{K_{i,\mathrm{R}} \, d_{i}}{V K_{i,\mathrm{R}} - L} = 1 \tag{20}$$
<p>The summation in Eq. (20) runs over all components, with the number of components defined in Table 1. No pinch composition appears in Eq. (20): the unknown pinch compositions have been eliminated, and Eq. (20) is a condition on flow rates, distribution coefficients, and product flows alone.</p>
<p>Step U5: introduction of the minimum reflux ratio. Division of the numerator and the denominator of every term of Eq. (20) by the distillate molar flow rate converts the distillate component flow into the distillate mole fraction, through the definition in Table 1. At the minimum reflux condition the reflux ratio takes the minimum value, and the rectifying-section total balance (the vapour flow equals the liquid flow plus the distillate flow) gives the vapour flow as the minimum reflux ratio plus one, multiplied by the distillate flow, while the liquid flow is the minimum reflux ratio multiplied by the distillate flow. Substitution of the two flow expressions gives:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{K_{i,\mathrm{R}} \, x_{D,i}}{(R_{\min} + 1) K_{i,\mathrm{R}} - R_{\min}} = 1 \tag{21}$$
<p>Step U6: introduction of relative volatilities and of the rectifying pinch parameter. By Assumption U2 and the definition in Table 1, the distribution coefficient of any component in the rectifying pinch zone equals the relative volatility of that component multiplied by the distribution coefficient of the reference component in the same zone. Substitution of that factorisation into Eq. (21), followed by factorisation of the denominator of each term as the product of three factors (the minimum reflux ratio plus one; the distribution coefficient of the reference component; and the difference between the relative volatility of the component and the rectifying pinch parameter of Table 1), cancels the distribution coefficient of the reference component between numerator and denominator and multiplies both sides by the minimum reflux ratio plus one. The result is the rectifying pinch relation:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{\alpha_{i} \, x_{D,i}}{\alpha_{i} - \theta_{\mathrm{R}}} = R_{\min} + 1 \tag{22}$$
<p>Equation (22) already has the form of the second Underwood equation, but with the rectifying pinch parameter in place of the Underwood parameter. The rectifying pinch parameter is not an additional physical quantity: the definition in Table 1 expresses the parameter through the minimum reflux ratio and the distribution coefficient of the reference component at the pinch, both of which are unknown before the calculation, so Eq. (22) is one equation in two unknowns and cannot be solved for the minimum reflux ratio until a second condition supplies the parameter.</p>
<p>Step U7: stripping pinch zone. The same four operations applied to the stripping section give the second condition in a parallel form. The equilibrium relation in the stripping pinch zone, the component balance over an envelope enclosing the reboiler and the column up to a cut in the stripping pinch zone, and the solution for the stripping pinch liquid composition are, in order:</p>
$$y_{i}^{\mathrm{S}} = K_{i,\mathrm{S}} \, x_{i}^{\mathrm{S}} \tag{23}$$
$$\bar{L} x_{i}^{\mathrm{S}} = \bar{V} y_{i}^{\mathrm{S}} + b_{i} \tag{24}$$
$$x_{i}^{\mathrm{S}} = \frac{b_{i}}{\bar{L} - \bar{V} K_{i,\mathrm{S}}} \tag{25}$$
<p>Vapour-phase closure in the stripping pinch zone, by the same summation as Step U4, gives:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{K_{i,\mathrm{S}} \, b_{i}}{\bar{L} - \bar{V} K_{i,\mathrm{S}}} = 1 \tag{26}$$
<p>Division of the numerator and the denominator of every term of Eq. (26) by the bottoms molar flow rate converts the bottoms component flow into the bottoms mole fraction. The stripping-section total balance (the liquid flow equals the vapour flow plus the bottoms flow) and the definition of the boilup ratio give the liquid flow as the boilup ratio plus one, multiplied by the bottoms flow, and the vapour flow as the boilup ratio multiplied by the bottoms flow. Substitution of the two flow expressions gives:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{K_{i,\mathrm{S}} \, x_{B,i}}{(R_{B} + 1) - R_{B} K_{i,\mathrm{S}}} = 1 \tag{27}$$
<p>Factorisation of the distribution coefficients in Eq. (27) through the relative volatilities, by the same operation as Step U6, with the stripping pinch parameter of Table 1 in place of the rectifying pinch parameter, gives the stripping pinch relation. The denominator of each term acquires a factor of the negative of the product of the boilup ratio and the distribution coefficient of the reference component, and the sign and the boilup ratio move to the right-hand side:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{\alpha_{i} \, x_{B,i}}{\alpha_{i} - \theta_{\mathrm{S}}} = -R_{B} \tag{28}$$
<p>Step U8: flow relations between the two sections. Summation of the overall component balance of Eq. (1) over all components, with each set of mole fractions summing to one by Assumption C4, gives the overall total balance: the feed molar flow rate equals the distillate molar flow rate plus the bottoms molar flow rate. The feed thermal condition parameter of Table 1 equals, under constant molar overflow, the increase in liquid molar flow rate across the feed stage per unit of feed flow rate, which states the liquid flow relation between the sections:</p>
$$\bar{L} = L + q F \tag{29}$$
<p>Substitution of the two section total balances into Eq. (29), replacement of the sum of the product flow rates by the feed flow rate through the overall total balance, and rearrangement give the vapour flow relation between the sections:</p>
$$V - \bar{V} = (1 - q) F \tag{30}$$
<p>Step U9: the common pinch parameter and the feed equation. Assumption U4 states that a single value of the pinch parameter satisfies the rectifying pinch relation of Eq. (22) and the stripping pinch relation of Eq. (28) simultaneously, so that the rectifying pinch parameter and the stripping pinch parameter are both replaced by the common Underwood parameter of Table 1. Assumption U4 is the substantive step of the Underwood method beyond balances, equilibrium, constant molar overflow, and constant relative volatilities: the two pinch parameters are defined through different pinch distribution coefficients and different section ratios, and equality of the two parameters is the condition that one operating state produces a pinch in both sections at the same time. For components that distribute between both products the condition is the basis of the method; components that appear in only one product require the additional treatment noted in the scope statement at the end of the present section.</p>
<p>With Assumption U4 in force, addition of the two pinch relations weighted by product flow rates proceeds as follows. Multiplication of Eq. (22), with the common parameter, by the distillate molar flow rate, and multiplication of Eq. (28), with the common parameter, by the bottoms molar flow rate, followed by addition of the two results and collection of the two terms for each component through the overall component balance of Eq. (1), gives:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{\alpha_{i} \, f_{i}}{\alpha_{i} - \theta} = D (R_{\min} + 1) - B R_{B} \tag{31}$$
<p>The right-hand side of Eq. (31) simplifies through the definitions of the two ratios: the distillate molar flow rate multiplied by the minimum reflux ratio plus one equals the rectifying vapour flow rate, and the bottoms molar flow rate multiplied by the boilup ratio equals the stripping vapour flow rate, so the right-hand side equals the difference of the two vapour flow rates. Replacement of that difference through Eq. (30), division of every term by the feed molar flow rate, and conversion of the feed component flow into the feed mole fraction through the definition in Table 1 give the first Underwood equation [2, 3]:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{\alpha_{i} \, z_{F,i}}{\alpha_{i} - \theta} = 1 - q \tag{32}$$
<p>Step U10: the second Underwood equation. Replacement of the rectifying pinch parameter in Eq. (22) by the common Underwood parameter under Assumption U4 gives the second Underwood equation:</p>
$$\sum_{i=1}^{C} \frac{\alpha_{i} \, x_{D,i}}{\alpha_{i} - \theta} = R_{\min} + 1 \tag{33}$$
<p>Equations (32) and (33) are the Underwood equations. The solution procedure is fixed by the structure of the derivation: Eq. (32) contains the feed composition, the feed thermal condition, and the relative volatilities, all of which are known, and the single unknown is the Underwood parameter; solution of Eq. (32) for the parameter therefore precedes all other steps. Substitution of the parameter into Eq. (33) then gives the minimum reflux ratio directly, from the distillate composition and the relative volatilities.</p>
<p>Root selection. Equation (32) is a rational equation in the Underwood parameter, with a pole at the relative volatility of each component. Between each pair of consecutive relative volatilities the left-hand side of Eq. (32) passes continuously from one infinite limit to the opposite infinite limit, so one root lies in each such interval, and multiplication of Eq. (32) out into polynomial form gives a polynomial of degree one less than the number of distinct relative volatilities. The root required for the minimum reflux calculation is the root lying between the relative volatility of the heavy key and the relative volatility of the light key; roots in other intervals correspond to pinch conditions between other pairs of adjacent components and govern separations between those components, not the specified key separation. Selection of a root outside the key interval is the most common numerical error in application of the method and produces a minimum reflux ratio for a different separation than the specified separation.</p>
<p>Scope and limitations of the Underwood result. The method is formulated for two classes of separation. In a class 1 separation every component distributes between both products, and Eqs. (32) and (33) apply with the specified product compositions as written. In a class 2 separation one or more components appear in only one product; the product composition of a non-distributing component is then not an independent specification, and the standard treatment retains Eqs. (32) and (33) with the key components and the distributing components evaluated as specified and the non-distributing components assigned entirely to the product in which the components appear. Further limitations follow from the assumptions: constant molar overflow fails for mixtures with strongly unequal molar latent heats; constant relative volatility fails for strongly non-ideal mixtures; and a pinch that controls the separation away from the feed stage, rather than at the feed stage as required by Assumption U3, is not represented by the derivation, in which case the Underwood value can underestimate the true minimum reflux ratio. The result is a minimum in the same sense as the Fenske result: operation below the minimum reflux ratio cannot achieve the specified separation with any number of stages, and operation at the minimum reflux ratio requires an infinite number of stages, so design reflux ratios are set as a multiple of the minimum value.</p>

<h2 id="example">Worked example</h2>
<p>Given data. A binary mixture of the light key and the heavy key is separated with the following specifications, in the symbols of Table 1: the relative volatility of the light key with respect to the heavy key is \(\alpha = 2.50\), constant throughout the column, so the relative volatility of the heavy key with respect to itself as reference component is \(\alpha_{\mathrm{HK}} = 1\); the feed mole fraction of the light key is \(z_{F,\mathrm{LK}} = 0.50\); the distillate mole fraction of the light key is \(x_{D,\mathrm{LK}} = 0.95\); the bottoms mole fraction of the light key is \(x_{B,\mathrm{LK}} = 0.05\); and the feed is saturated liquid, so the feed thermal condition parameter is \(q = 1\). The heavy-key mole fraction in each stream is one minus the light-key mole fraction in the same stream.</p>
<p>Fenske calculation. The distillate enrichment ratio is \(0.95/0.05 = 19.0\). The bottoms enrichment ratio, heavy key to light key, is \(0.95/0.05 = 19.0\). The product of the two ratios in Eq. (12) is \(19.0 \times 19.0 = 361\). The natural logarithm of the product is \(\ln 361 = 5.889\) and the natural logarithm of the relative volatility is \(\ln 2.50 = 0.9163\). Equation (12) therefore gives:</p>
$$N_{\min} = \frac{5.889}{0.9163} = 6.43 \tag{34}$$
<p>The minimum number of equilibrium stages is 6.43, counting the reboiler as one stage under the convention of Table 1. With a total condenser, which contributes no stage, the number of equilibrium stages inside the column is \(6.43 - 1 = 5.43\), in addition to the reboiler. The fractional stage count is a property of the limiting calculation: a real column provides a whole number of stages and operates at finite reflux, and the fractional value states that six stages in total fall short of the specified separation at total reflux while seven stages exceed the requirement at total reflux. As an arithmetic check, the relative volatility raised to the power of the minimum stage count returns the enrichment product: \(2.50^{6.43} = 361\).</p>
<p>Underwood calculation, Step 1: solution of the feed equation for the Underwood parameter. Substitution of the given data into Eq. (32), with the right-hand side equal to zero because the feed thermal condition parameter equals one, gives:</p>
$$\frac{2.50 \times 0.50}{2.50 - \theta} + \frac{1 \times 0.50}{1 - \theta} = 0 \tag{35}$$
<p>Evaluation of the two numerator products, movement of the second term to the right-hand side with a change of sign, and replacement of the denominator of the moved term by its negative give \(1.25/(2.50 - \theta) = 0.50/(\theta - 1)\). Cross-multiplication gives \(1.25 \, (\theta - 1) = 0.50 \, (2.50 - \theta)\). Expansion of both sides gives \(1.25 \, \theta - 1.25 = 1.25 - 0.50 \, \theta\). Collection of the terms containing the Underwood parameter on the left-hand side and of the constant terms on the right-hand side gives \(1.75 \, \theta = 2.50\), and division by 1.75 gives:</p>
$$\theta = 1.429 \tag{36}$$
<p>The value of Eq. (36) lies between the relative volatility of the heavy key, which is 1, and the relative volatility of the light key, which is 2.50, as required by the root-selection rule of the Underwood derivation. The exact value is the fraction 10/7.</p>
<p>Underwood calculation, Step 2: minimum reflux ratio. Substitution of the Underwood parameter from Eq. (36) and of the distillate composition into Eq. (33) gives the minimum reflux ratio plus one as the sum of two terms. The light-key term is \(2.50 \times 0.95/(2.50 - 1.429) = 2.375/1.071 = 2.217\). The heavy-key term is \(1 \times 0.05/(1 - 1.429) = 0.05/(-0.429) = -0.117\). The sum of the two terms is \(2.217 - 0.117 = 2.100\), so:</p>
$$R_{\min} = 2.100 - 1 = 1.10 \tag{37}$$
<p>Independent check by the binary graphical construction. For a binary mixture with constant relative volatility, the equilibrium relation between the vapour mole fraction of the light key and the liquid mole fraction of the light key follows from Eq. (4) written for the two components and solved for the vapour mole fraction:</p>
$$y = \frac{\alpha x}{1 + (\alpha - 1) x} \tag{38}$$
<p>For saturated liquid feed the feed line is vertical at the feed composition, so the pinch point of the graphical construction lies at the intersection of the feed line with the equilibrium curve of Eq. (38). At the feed composition of 0.50, Eq. (38) gives a vapour mole fraction of \(2.50 \times 0.50/(1 + 1.50 \times 0.50) = 1.25/1.75 = 0.714\). The rectifying operating line at minimum reflux passes through the distillate point, at which both compositions equal 0.95, and through the pinch point just calculated. The slope of the rectifying operating line is the ratio of the liquid flow rate to the vapour flow rate in the rectifying section, which the definitions of Table 1 and the rectifying total balance express as the reflux ratio divided by the reflux ratio plus one. The slope from the two points is \((0.95 - 0.714)/(0.95 - 0.50) = 0.524\). Setting the slope expression equal to the computed slope and solving for the reflux ratio gives \(0.524/(1 - 0.524) = 1.10\), in agreement with Eq. (37). The graphical construction uses none of the Underwood algebra, so the agreement verifies the Underwood result for the binary case.</p>
