<p>Check of the stripping pinch relation and of the common parameter. Substitution of the Underwood parameter from Eq. (36) and of the bottoms composition into the stripping pinch relation of Eq. (28) gives a light-key term of \(2.50 \times 0.05/(2.50 - 1.429) = 0.117\) and a heavy-key term of \(1 \times 0.95/(1 - 1.429) = -2.217\), with a sum of \(-2.100\). The right-hand side of Eq. (28) is the negative of the boilup ratio at the minimum reflux condition. At the minimum reflux condition with saturated liquid feed, the stripping vapour flow rate equals the rectifying vapour flow rate, the rectifying vapour flow rate equals the minimum reflux ratio plus one multiplied by the distillate flow rate, and the overall balances give equal distillate and bottoms flow rates (each one half of the feed flow rate, from the overall component balance at the stated compositions). The boilup ratio is therefore \(2.100 \times 1 = 2.100\), and the stripping relation is satisfied by the same parameter value as the rectifying relation. The common-parameter assumption of Step U9 is verified numerically for the worked example.</p>
<p>Variation with feed thermal condition. Repetition of the Underwood calculation at the same compositions and the same relative volatility, for three values of the feed thermal condition parameter, gives the results of Table 2. For the two cases other than saturated liquid feed, the Underwood parameter was obtained by numerical solution of Eq. (32), because the feed equation no longer reduces to a linear equation in the parameter.</p>
